## Proof

**The operator.** Define $T \in \mathcal{B}(\ell^2)$ by
$$T(x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4, \dots) = (-x_2, x_1, -x_4, x_3, \dots).$$
$T$ is an isometry since $\|Tx\|^2 = |x_2|^2+|x_1|^2+|x_4|^2+|x_3|^2+\cdots = \|x\|^2$, so $\|T\|=1$ and indeed $T\in\mathcal{B}(\ell^2)$.

**Key identity.** $T$ acts on each consecutive pair $(x_{2k-1},x_{2k})$ by the $2\times 2$ matrix $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$. Since $J^2=-I_2$, applying $T$ twice gives
$$T^2(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4,\dots) = T(-x_2,x_1,-x_4,x_3,\dots) = (-x_1,-x_2,-x_3,-x_4,\dots) = -x.$$
Hence
$$T^2 = -I. \tag{1}$$

**Spectrum is contained in $\{i,-i\}$.** From (1), $T$ satisfies the polynomial $p(\lambda)=\lambda^2+1$. By the spectral mapping property for polynomials, $p(\sigma(T))\subseteq\sigma(p(T))=\sigma(-I)=\{-1\}$. Thus for every $\lambda\in\sigma(T)$, $\lambda^2+1=0$, i.e. $\lambda\in\{i,-i\}$. Therefore
$$\sigma(T)\subseteq\{i,-i\}.$$

**Both $i$ and $-i$ are eigenvalues.** 

*Eigenvalue $i$:* Take $u=(i,1,0,0,\dots)\in\ell^2$. Then
$$Tu = (-1,\, i,\, 0,\, 0,\dots) = i\,(i,\,1,\,0,\,0,\dots) = i\,u.$$
So $i\in\sigma_p(T)\subseteq\sigma(T)$.

*Eigenvalue $-i$:* Take $v=(1,i,0,0,\dots)\in\ell^2$. Then
$$Tv = (-i,\,1,\,0,\,0,\dots) = -i\,(1,\,i,\,0,\,0,\dots) = -i\,v.$$
So $-i\in\sigma_p(T)\subseteq\sigma(T)$.

(More generally, every vector supported on a single block $(x_{2k-1},x_{2k})$ with $(x_{2k-1},x_{2k})\in\operatorname{span}\{(i,1)\}$ is an eigenvector for $i$, and similarly for $-i$; the eigenspaces are infinite-dimensional.)

**Conclusion.** Combining the containment $\sigma(T)\subseteq\{i,-i\}$ with the fact that both points are eigenvalues,
$$\boxed{\sigma(T)=\sigma_p(T)=\{i,\,-i\}}.$$
There is no continuous or residual spectrum: the spectrum consists entirely of eigenvalues, each of infinite multiplicity.

